    bridge: 'Terminology can vary; confirm the company’s reporting definition.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    indianContext: 'Operating profit is the number leadership teams in India scrutinise to judge how well the core business is being run day to day, stripped of the noise from interest costs, tax and one-off items, which can vary a lot by how a company is financed. Because the exact line items included can differ between companies, finance teams are often asked to clarify how a particular firm defines it before comparisons are made across businesses.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It is comparable to assessing how well a formation performed its core assigned task, independent of how it was funded or resourced from outside — separating the quality of execution from the terms of the budget or logistics support it was given. This lets you judge operational competence on its own merits.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Operating profit looks strong this year, even though net profit took a hit from the interest on the new loan."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers sometimes conflate operating profit with the company\'s final bottom-line profit, not realising that financing costs and taxes — which can swing the final number significantly — are deliberately excluded from this measure.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Net Profit',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Profit remaining after relevant operating, financing, tax and other expenses.',
    whereSeen: 'P&L, annual reports',
    bridge: 'Often synonymous with profit after tax in everyday discussion.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'Net profit is the headline number quoted in Indian annual reports and board discussions because it is, in everyday language, what shareholders and employees think of as "the profit," after every operating, financing and tax expense has been accounted for. It shapes decisions from bonus pools to dividend payouts to how confident leadership feels about further investment.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is the equivalent of the final net gain after every operation, every logistics cost, every casualty and every resource spent has been tallied for the campaign as a whole — the true bottom-line outcome rather than any single battle\'s result. It is the number that ultimately determines whether the entire campaign was worth the resources committed.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Revenue was up, but net profit actually fell because of the one-time settlement cost we booked this quarter."',
    trapForOfficers: 'A common mistake is assuming a company reporting strong revenue must also be strongly profitable, when in fact net profit — after all expenses — can be thin or even negative despite healthy top-line numbers.',
