  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Working Capital',
    fullForm: 'Current Assets minus Current Liabilities',
    meaning: 'Resources tied up in day-to-day operations, especially receivables, inventory and payables.',
    whereSeen: 'Finance, operations',
    bridge: 'A key link between operational activity and cash.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'Working capital is closely tracked by Indian finance and operations teams because it shows how much cash is effectively locked up in the everyday cycle of holding inventory, waiting for customers to pay, and paying suppliers — a real constraint on how fast a business can grow without needing new funding. Getting this cycle tight is often a bigger lever on cash health than profit itself, especially for manufacturing and trading businesses.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is like the stores and matériel tied up in the forward supply chain at any given moment — not lost, but not available for anything else either until they move through the pipeline. A commander managing a long, slow supply line understands this constraint instinctively, even if the terminology is different.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We\'re profitable on paper, but our working capital is stuck in unpaid customer invoices and unsold stock."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers new to business often assume that if a company is profitable, it must automatically have cash on hand, missing how working capital tied up in receivables and inventory can leave a profitable company genuinely short of usable cash.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Accounts Receivable (AR)',
    fullForm: 'Accounts Receivable',
    meaning: 'Money customers owe the company for goods/services already delivered.',
    whereSeen: 'Finance, collections',
    bridge: 'Customer credit outstanding.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'AR is a constant conversation in Indian sales and finance teams because customers, especially in B2B and government-linked business, often take long to pay, and the collections team\'s job is essentially to convert this on-paper asset into real cash. High AR relative to sales is often an early warning sign finance teams flag during reviews.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Think of it as supplies or support promised by an ally or another formation that hasn\'t yet arrived — recorded as expected, but not something you can actually use until it\'s physically in hand. Planning around it without confirming delivery timelines is risky.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our AR has ballooned this quarter — we need to chase these big accounts before it turns into a cash problem."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might treat a large accounts receivable balance as good news because it represents sales made, without recognising that until that money is actually collected, it is a risk rather than usable cash.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Accounts Payable (AP)',
    fullForm: 'Accounts Payable',
    meaning: 'Money the company owes suppliers for goods/services received.',
    whereSeen: 'Finance, procurement',
    bridge: 'Supplier obligations.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'AP represents what a company owes its vendors, and Indian procurement and finance teams actively manage payment timing on this line — paying too fast can strain cash, paying too slow can damage supplier relationships or invite penalties, so it is a genuine balancing act discussed regularly in vendor and treasury meetings.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is like outstanding obligations owed to a support unit or contractor for services already rendered — you have use of what they provided, but settlement is still pending, and timing that settlement well matters for maintaining the relationship going forward.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Let\'s stretch AP a little this month to manage cash, but not so much that we damage supplier trust."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers sometimes assume unpaid supplier bills are simply a problem to be resolved as fast as possible, not realising that the timing of accounts payable is a deliberate cash management lever many finance teams actively manage rather than an oversight to fix immediately.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Cash Flow',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Movement of cash into and out of the business.',
    whereSeen: 'Finance, treasury, leadership',
    bridge: 'Profit can exist without equivalent cash at the same time.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'Cash flow gets separate, dedicated attention from Indian treasury and leadership teams because a company can show a profit on paper while genuinely struggling to pay salaries or vendors if cash isn\'t moving in at the same pace expenses go out. This is why cash flow statements sit alongside profit numbers in almost every serious finance review.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is like the actual physical movement of fuel, rations and ammunition through the supply chain in real time, as distinct from what\'s been requisitioned or promised on paper — what matters operationally is what\'s actually arriving and departing, not what the ledger says should be there.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"The P&L looks fine, but cash flow is tight this month because two large customer payments are delayed."',
    trapForOfficers: 'A frequent misunderstanding is equating profit with available cash — an officer might see a profitable P&L and assume the company has plenty of money on hand, when the timing of actual cash movements can tell a very different story.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Free Cash Flow (FCF)',
    fullForm: 'Free Cash Flow',
    meaning: 'Cash generated after the spending needed to maintain or build the business, with exact definition varying by analysis.',
    whereSeen: 'Finance, valuation',
    bridge: 'Important for understanding cash available to investors or reinvestment.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    indianContext: 'FCF matters to Indian finance teams and investors because it shows what cash is genuinely left over after the business has spent what it needs to keep running and growing, which is the real pool available for paying down debt, rewarding shareholders or funding new initiatives. It is treated as a more honest indicator of financial health than profit alone in many valuation discussions.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is comparable to the surplus capacity or resources a unit has left after meeting all its essential operational and maintenance commitments — what\'s genuinely available for new initiatives, rather than what merely appears in the stores ledger before real obligations are met.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Net profit looks decent, but free cash flow is what we should present to the board since it shows what we can actually reinvest."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may assume free cash flow is simply another name for profit, missing that it specifically accounts for capital spending and reinvestment needs, which can make it very different from the net profit figure on the P&L.',
