  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Balance Sheet',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'A statement of assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time.',
    whereSeen: 'Finance, annual reports',
    bridge: 'A snapshot of financial position.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    indianContext: 'The balance sheet is the document Indian finance teams and boards use to understand a company\'s financial position at a specific moment — what it owns, what it owes and what belongs to shareholders — and it\'s a standard reference point in annual reports, loan applications and investor discussions. Unlike the P&L, which covers a period, the balance sheet is a snapshot, and finance teams are careful to explain that distinction to non-finance colleagues.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is like a complete inventory and liability report of a formation taken on a single day — everything held, everything owed to others, and everything genuinely belonging to the unit, captured as a fixed point-in-time picture rather than a running log of activity.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Before we approve the new loan, let\'s look at how it changes the balance sheet, not just this year\'s P&L."',
    trapForOfficers: 'A newcomer might confuse the balance sheet with the P&L and expect it to show revenue or profit for the year, not realising it is a snapshot of assets, liabilities and equity at one point in time rather than a record of activity over a period.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'PBT',
    fullForm: 'Profit Before Tax',
    meaning: 'Profit before income tax expense.',
    whereSeen: 'P&L, finance',
    bridge: 'Useful for comparing profitability before tax.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    indianContext: 'Indian finance teams use PBT to compare profitability across companies or periods on a basis unaffected by differing tax situations, since tax rates, exemptions and one-time tax items can distort comparisons if only looking at profit after tax. It\'s a common line item discussed just before the final net profit figure in P&L reviews.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is like assessing an operation\'s outcome before accounting for the toll of external, largely unavoidable overheads outside the operation\'s own control — giving a cleaner read on how the operation itself performed, independent of factors imposed from outside.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"PBT growth is solid this year, though the effective tax rate change will bring net profit growth down a bit."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may not immediately grasp why finance teams separate PBT from net profit, and might assume the two numbers should be roughly the same, missing how significantly tax charges can move the final bottom line.',
