    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Accounting allocation of the cost of a tangible asset over its useful life.',
    whereSeen: 'Finance, P&L',
    bridge: 'An accounting expense; not itself a current-period cash payment.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    indianContext: 'Depreciation shows up in every Indian company\'s P&L as a non-cash expense that spreads the cost of equipment, vehicles or buildings over their useful life, and finance teams routinely explain to operational managers why a profitable-looking unit can still show a large depreciation charge dragging down reported profit. It\'s a recurring topic when discussing capital-heavy businesses like manufacturing or infrastructure.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is like accounting for the gradual wear and eventual replacement cost of a vehicle fleet or heavy equipment over its service life, rather than charging the full purchase cost against the day it was bought — spreading the real economic cost across the years it\'s actually used.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our profit took a hit this quarter, but a large chunk of that is just depreciation on the new plant, not an actual cash cost."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers sometimes treat depreciation as a real cash outflow the company is currently paying, not realising it is an accounting allocation of a past purchase, so a company can have healthy cash reserves even while depreciation is dragging down reported profit.',
  ),
