    bridge: 'Helps compare investments by considering time value of money.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    priorityTier: 3,
    indianContext: 'Indian corporate finance and capital investment teams use NPV to decide whether a project is worth funding, by translating future expected cash flows into today\'s value and comparing that against the upfront investment required. It routinely appears in board papers when large capital expenditure, plant expansion or acquisition decisions are being evaluated.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is akin to weighing whether a long, resource-intensive campaign is worth launching today, given that resources committed now are more certain and valuable than promised gains far in the future — a way of discounting uncertain future payoff against present cost.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"The NPV on this expansion is positive even after discounting, so finance is comfortable recommending it to the board."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers new to finance sometimes treat all future cash inflows as equally valuable to money today, missing the core idea behind NPV that a rupee received years from now is worth meaningfully less than a rupee in hand today.',
