    bridge: 'Used in many DCF/valuation analyses.',
    category: 'Finance & Accounting',
    priorityTier: 3,
    indianContext: 'Indian corporate finance teams use WACC as the benchmark hurdle rate a project or investment must clear to be worth pursuing, since it reflects the blended cost of the debt and equity capital funding the company. It\'s referenced constantly in valuation models and capital allocation debates, often as a number handed down from finance rather than something operational managers calculate themselves.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is comparable to the baseline cost of maintaining and equipping a force from all its combined funding sources — any new operation needs to deliver enough value to justify drawing on that same pool of resources rather than leaving it untouched.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"If this project\'s expected return doesn\'t clear our WACC, it\'s actually destroying value even if it looks profitable on paper."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers might assume any project showing a positive return is automatically worth doing, without realising that the return needs to exceed the company\'s cost of capital, or WACC, to actually be creating value rather than just breaking even on funding costs.',
