  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'CRM',
    fullForm: 'Customer Relationship Management',
    meaning: 'System/process used to manage customer information, interactions, opportunities and sales activity.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, customer success, dashboards',
    bridge: 'Think of it as the structured customer/opportunity record.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'In most Indian companies, the CRM is the single source of truth that sales, marketing and leadership all look at before a review meeting — nobody trusts a rep\'s verbal claim of "the deal is progressing" unless it\'s logged there. Sales managers use it to track individual and team pipelines, forecast the month\'s or quarter\'s numbers, and decide where to apply pressure, while customer success teams use the same record to see the full history before a renewal call.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It functions much like a unit\'s operational log or contact register — a running, disciplined record of who you\'ve engaged, what was discussed, and what the next action is, so that continuity survives even when the person handling the relationship changes. Just as a good ops room insists every contact and status update gets logged rather than kept in someone\'s head, a CRM only works if the discipline of entering data is maintained.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Has that been updated in the CRM? If it\'s not in the system, as far as the monthly forecast is concerned, it doesn\'t exist."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer used to keeping detailed situational awareness mentally or in a personal notebook may under-log activity in the CRM, not realising that in corporate settings the system itself — not the individual\'s memory — is treated as the official record for forecasting and accountability.',
