  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Lead',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'A person or organisation that may have potential interest in the company’s offering.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales funnel',
    bridge: 'An early-stage sales opportunity.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'Sales teams generate leads from multiple sources — inbound website enquiries, trade shows, referrals, cold outreach — and the first job of a junior sales or inside-sales person is often simply to qualify which leads are worth pursuing versus which are time-wasters. Leadership tracks lead volume closely because it\'s the earliest indicator of whether marketing and sales activity is producing anything at all.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'A lead is comparable to an unconfirmed sighting or a raw piece of intelligence — it signals a possible point of interest, but it hasn\'t yet been verified, assessed for credibility, or acted upon. Just as not every report from the field turns into an actionable target, not every lead turns into a real sale.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We got forty leads from the exhibition last week — someone needs to call each one and figure out which are actually worth chasing."',
    trapForOfficers: 'A newly transitioned officer might treat every lead as equally worth immediate, full effort, not realising that experienced sales teams deliberately triage leads quickly and drop the weak ones rather than pursuing everything with equal intensity.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Prospect',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'A lead that has been qualified as a plausible potential customer.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales reviews',
    bridge: 'Further developed than a raw lead.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'Once a lead clears an initial qualification filter — budget, need, authority to buy — it gets labelled a prospect, and sales reviews typically ask reps to justify why each prospect on their list is genuinely worth the time being invested. Managers use the term to separate serious pursuit from speculative outreach, and it often determines who gets scarce resources like a senior colleague\'s time on a client call.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Think of it as the step after a raw sighting has been assessed and confirmed as a credible point of interest worth committing resources to track further — still short of a fully developed, actionable plan, but no longer just noise. It\'s the difference between everything on the horizon and the specific contacts you\'ve decided are worth a closer look.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"That\'s not a real prospect yet, it\'s still a lead — call them first and confirm budget before we put them on the prospect list."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers sometimes use "lead" and "prospect" interchangeably, but in sales meetings the distinction is deliberate — using the wrong term can make it look like a deal is further along (or less developed) than it actually is, which affects how much attention and resource it gets.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Opportunity',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'A qualified potential sale being actively pursued.',
    whereSeen: 'CRM, pipeline reviews',
    bridge: 'Usually has a defined value, customer and expected close path.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'An opportunity is what actually shows up as a line item in the sales pipeline with an estimated deal value, an expected close date and a named decision-maker, and it\'s the unit that revenue forecasts are built from. Sales leaders scrutinise the opportunity list every week to challenge whether the stated value and close date are realistic or just wishful thinking by the rep.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is closest to a developed target that has moved from possibility to an actual plan with defined resources, a timeline and expected outcome — the point where you\'d commit assets because the picture is clear enough to act on. Just as a commander wants specifics — what, when, with what resources — before committing to an operation, a sales leader wants specifics before an opportunity is taken seriously in the forecast.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"I\'ll only count it as an opportunity in this quarter\'s forecast once we have a value and a realistic close date, not just a friendly conversation."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer new to sales may report progress in qualitative terms ("good meeting, they seemed interested") without pinning down the concrete figures — value, stage, close date — that turn a conversation into a countable opportunity in the pipeline.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Pipeline',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'The set of active sales opportunities at different stages.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales reviews',
    bridge: 'A forward-looking view of potential revenue.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'The pipeline is the aggregated view of every open opportunity a salesperson or team is working, broken down by stage, and it\'s the primary tool sales leadership uses to forecast whether the quarter\'s or year\'s revenue target will be met. A "healthy pipeline" usually means enough total value at various stages to comfortably exceed target even after accounting for deals that will inevitably fall through.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It resembles the overall picture of ongoing operations at different stages of readiness — some just initiated, some mid-execution, some nearly complete — that a commander reviews to judge whether enough is in motion to achieve the larger objective. A thin pipeline is like having too few operations in progress to guarantee mission success even if most of them succeed.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our pipeline for this quarter looks thin — we need more opportunities in the early stages, not just the two big deals near closing."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might equate a long pipeline list with strong performance, without grasping that experienced sales managers discount pipeline value heavily by stage and probability — a pipeline full of early-stage, unqualified opportunities is far weaker than it looks on paper.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Funnel',
    fullForm: 'Sales Funnel',
    meaning: 'Stages through which potential customers move from awareness/interest to purchase.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, marketing',
    bridge: 'Used to understand conversion and leakage.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'Marketing and sales teams map the funnel to understand exactly where potential customers are dropping off — from initial awareness, through interest and consideration, to actual purchase — and most reviews focus on improving the weakest stage rather than the whole process at once. It\'s a shared visual language between marketing (who owns the top of the funnel) and sales (who owns the bottom), and disagreements about whose stage is underperforming are common.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Picture it like the stages a large intake of resources or personnel goes through before only a smaller, fully ready subset remains fit for the final task — attrition happens at each stage, and understanding where the biggest drop-off occurs tells you where to invest effort. Analysing funnel leakage is like analysing at which stage of a selection or training pipeline you lose the most candidates, and why.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our funnel shows plenty of people at the top, but conversion drops badly between the demo stage and the proposal stage — that\'s where we\'re losing them."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers sometimes assume the funnel is purely a sales problem to fix with more calls, missing that a leaky funnel is often a marketing, pricing, or product issue best diagnosed stage-by-stage rather than solved with generic extra effort.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Conversion Rate',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Percentage of people/opportunities that progress to the desired next action.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, marketing, digital',
    bridge: 'A key effectiveness metric.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'Conversion rate gets quoted at almost every stage of business — website visitors to sign-ups, leads to opportunities, opportunities to closed deals — and comparing conversion rate across reps, channels or time periods is one of the most common ways performance gets judged in reviews. A small change in conversion rate can matter more to leadership than a large change in raw activity volume, because it signals efficiency, not just effort.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It\'s analogous to a hit rate or success ratio for a repeated activity — the proportion of attempts, engagements or sorties that actually achieve the intended effect, which is often a better indicator of quality than the raw number of attempts. A high volume of activity with a low conversion/success rate points to a process or targeting problem, not an effort problem.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We doubled the number of calls this month, but if conversion rate stays flat that just means we\'re doing more of the same thing badly, not better."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might focus on maximising activity volume (more calls, more meetings) as a sign of hard work, without realising that in corporate settings a low conversion rate on high volume is often read as a sign of poor targeting or execution, not diligence.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'KAM',
    fullForm: 'Key Account Management / Key Account Manager',
    meaning: 'Management of strategically important customers; also refers to the role responsible for them.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, B2B',
    bridge: 'Focuses on retaining and growing important accounts.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'Key Account Management is applied to the handful of clients that generate a disproportionate share of revenue or strategic value, and a KAM is expected to build a multi-year relationship, understand the client\'s business deeply, and defend the account against competitors rather than simply chase the next transaction. In B2B firms, the KAM role is often a senior, trusted position because losing a key account can materially affect the company\'s numbers for the year.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This maps to the sustained, dedicated attention given to a critical asset or key relationship that the organisation cannot afford to lose — closer to how a liaison officer manages an important inter-service or coalition relationship over time, building trust and continuity rather than treating each interaction as a one-off. The KAM\'s job is protecting and growing a relationship, not winning a single engagement.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"That account needs a dedicated KAM now — it\'s too important to leave to whoever happens to pick up the phone."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might treat every client interaction with equal formality and process, not recognising that KAM work is deliberately relationship-heavy and requires investing disproportionate time in a small number of accounts rather than spreading attention evenly.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Upsell',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Selling a higher-value version or greater quantity of an existing offering.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, customer success',
    bridge: 'Increases value of an existing customer relationship.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'Sales and customer success teams are often measured on upsell revenue separately from new-customer revenue, because it\'s generally cheaper to sell more to an existing customer than to acquire a new one. A renewal conversation is frequently also an upsell conversation, where the account team pitches a higher tier, more licenses, or premium features based on the customer\'s actual usage patterns.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It resembles proposing an upgraded or expanded scope of support to a unit that already trusts and relies on your existing capability — offering more of what has already proven valuable, rather than trying to win over an entirely new client from scratch. The relationship and credibility built earlier is what makes the upsell conversation possible.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Before we chase new logos, let\'s look at the upsell potential in our existing base — some of these accounts are ready for the higher tier."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might see pushing additional purchases onto an already-paying customer as uncomfortably close to overselling, not recognising that a well-timed upsell based on genuine usage data is standard, expected account-management practice rather than a pushy sales tactic.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Cross-sell',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Selling a related product/service to an existing customer.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, customer success',
    bridge: 'Expands the customer relationship.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'Cross-selling is common wherever a company has more than one product line — a bank cross-selling insurance to a loan customer, or a software firm cross-selling a second module to a client already using the first. Account teams are often given specific cross-sell targets separate from their core renewal or upsell numbers, and it requires understanding the customer\'s broader needs, not just the product they already bought.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Think of it as recommending a complementary capability or support service to someone who already trusts your primary offering — like a logistics unit that, having built credibility on transport support, is then asked to also take on related maintenance support for the same formation. Existing trust in one area opens the door to adjacent areas.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"They\'re happy with the core product — this is a good moment to cross-sell the analytics add-on rather than wait for the renewal cycle."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might confuse cross-sell with upsell, using the terms interchangeably in a report or meeting, when managers expect precision — upsell means more of the same thing, cross-sell means a different, related product.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Churn',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Loss of customers or recurring revenue over a period.',
    whereSeen: 'SaaS, telecom, subscriptions',
    bridge: 'A critical retention metric.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'Churn is watched obsessively in subscription businesses like SaaS and telecom, because losing existing customers quietly erodes revenue even while new sales look strong, and a company can grow its top-line bookings while still shrinking if churn is high enough. Customer success teams are often specifically tasked with reducing churn, and a spike in churn triggers root-cause reviews at leadership level.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It\'s comparable to attrition in a unit\'s strength over time — personnel or assets leaving faster than they\'re replaced, which can quietly hollow out overall capability even if recruitment numbers look healthy on paper. Just as a commander tracks retention alongside intake, a business tracks churn alongside new sales.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our new sales numbers look great this quarter, but churn is up too — net, we might actually be shrinking."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might focus reporting and effort entirely on new customer acquisition, not realising that in many corporate reviews, controlling churn among existing customers is treated as equally or more important than winning new ones.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'CAC',
    fullForm: 'Customer Acquisition Cost',
    meaning: 'Average cost of acquiring a new customer, depending on the company’s calculation.',
    whereSeen: 'Marketing, start-ups, SaaS',
    bridge: 'Often compared with customer lifetime value.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'Customer Acquisition Cost is closely tracked by marketing and finance leadership together, since it forces a discipline of asking whether the sales and marketing spend to win a customer actually makes sense against what that customer is likely to be worth. Start-ups in particular get pressed hard on their CAC trend during investor and board reviews, because a rising CAC often signals that easy, cheap growth channels have been exhausted.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is akin to the total resource cost of successfully bringing in and onboarding a new recruit or asset — factoring in outreach, screening, training and induction — which a resource planner compares against how long and how usefully that recruit is expected to serve. A high cost to acquire someone only makes sense if their expected period of service justifies it.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our team\'s CAC crept up this quarter because we leaned harder on paid channels — we need to check whether the payback still makes sense."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might treat marketing and sales spend purely as a cost to be minimised, without grasping that CAC is meant to be judged relative to the value a customer brings back over time, not as an absolute number to drive down at all costs.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'LTV / CLV',
    fullForm: 'Lifetime Value / Customer Lifetime Value',
    meaning: 'Estimated economic value a customer generates over the relationship.',
    whereSeen: 'Marketing, SaaS, strategy',
    bridge: 'Useful for judging acquisition economics.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'LTV is almost always discussed alongside CAC, because the ratio between the two tells a company whether its growth engine is fundamentally sound — a low LTV relative to CAC is a warning sign that gets raised in strategy and investor conversations well before it shows up as a cash crisis. Different teams calculate LTV differently depending on assumptions about retention and margin, so the number itself is often debated as much as used.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It parallels estimating the total value an asset or trained individual will deliver over their full period of productive service, which is what justifies the upfront investment made in acquiring or training them in the first place. Just as training investment is judged against expected years of useful service, acquisition spend is judged against expected years of customer value.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"If LTV to CAC is below three, most investors will ask hard questions about whether this growth is actually profitable."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might treat LTV as a precise, settled figure, not realising it\'s usually a modelled estimate built on assumptions about future retention and margin — assumptions that different teams in the same company can and do disagree on.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'NPS',
    fullForm: 'Net Promoter Score',
    meaning: 'A customer loyalty metric based on willingness to recommend a company/product.',
    whereSeen: 'Customer experience, leadership',
    bridge: 'A survey metric; useful but not a complete measure of customer health.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'NPS surveys — "how likely are you to recommend us" — get sent to customers after key milestones, and leadership often tracks the trend over time as a proxy for overall customer sentiment, even while acknowledging its limitations. It\'s frequently cited in board decks and all-hands meetings as a single headline number, even though the underlying qualitative comments usually matter more than the score itself.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It resembles gauging general morale and confidence through informal soundings across a unit rather than a formal, detailed audit — a useful early-warning signal about sentiment, but not a substitute for actually investigating specific complaints or issues raised. A dip in NPS, like a dip in morale, tells you something is wrong without telling you exactly what.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"NPS dropped five points this quarter — before we panic, let\'s actually read the comments and see what\'s driving it."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might treat the single NPS number as a definitive verdict on customer health, missing that experienced teams treat it as a directional signal to investigate further rather than a precise measurement to act on literally.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'CSAT',
    fullForm: 'Customer Satisfaction Score',
    meaning: 'A customer satisfaction measure usually collected after an interaction or service event.',
    whereSeen: 'Customer service, operations',
    bridge: 'Often tied to a specific experience.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'CSAT is collected right after a specific interaction — a support call, a service visit, a delivery — and is used mainly to evaluate the quality of that particular touchpoint rather than the overall relationship. Operations and customer service teams often tie individual or team performance reviews partly to CSAT trends, making it a more immediate, granular metric than NPS.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Think of it as an after-action feedback check on a specific, discrete task just completed — did this particular support or logistics action meet the immediate need — rather than a broader assessment of the overall relationship or campaign. It\'s feedback on one interaction, not the whole engagement.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"CSAT on that support ticket came back low — let\'s pull the transcript and see exactly what went wrong in that one interaction."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might confuse CSAT with NPS since both are customer feedback scores, not realising CSAT is tied to a single specific interaction while NPS reflects broader overall loyalty — mixing them up in a report can misdirect where a problem is actually located.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Market Share',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'A company’s sales or volume as a proportion of the total relevant market.',
    whereSeen: 'Strategy, sales',
    bridge: 'Requires a clearly defined market and measurement basis.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'Market share comes up constantly in strategy discussions to frame whether a company is winning or losing relative to competitors, but the number is only meaningful once everyone agrees on how the "market" is defined — by geography, product category or customer segment — which is itself often a point of internal debate. Leadership sometimes chases market share growth even at the cost of short-term profitability, particularly in categories seen as strategically important.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It\'s comparable to assessing your relative strength or area of control compared to competing forces within a defined theatre — the assessment only makes sense once the boundaries of that theatre are clearly and consistently defined. Claiming ground without agreeing on the map first leads to disputes about what was actually gained.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Before we claim we\'ve grown market share, let\'s agree on what market we\'re actually measuring against — the numbers change a lot depending on that."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might accept a market share figure at face value without probing how the "market" was defined, missing that different definitions can make the same company look dominant or marginal.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'B2B',
    fullForm: 'Business to Business',
    meaning: 'Business selling to another business.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, marketing',
    bridge: 'Often involves longer buying cycles and multiple decision-makers.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'B2B businesses — enterprise software, industrial equipment, corporate services — typically involve longer sales cycles, multiple stakeholders on the buying side, and formal procurement processes, so B2B sales roles are structured very differently from consumer-facing ones. Understanding whether a company is B2B or B2C shapes almost everything about how its sales, marketing and even org structure are set up.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It resembles engagements conducted formally between organisations — such as inter-unit or inter-service coordination — where decisions go through multiple layers of approval and relationships are built over a long, structured process rather than a single quick transaction. Deals in this mode move at institutional pace, not individual pace.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"This is a B2B sale, so expect procurement, legal and the CFO\'s office to all weigh in before anything gets signed — it won\'t close in one meeting."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might apply consumer-sales instincts — a single decisive pitch to one person — to a B2B deal, underestimating how many stakeholders and approval layers actually need to be satisfied before the deal closes.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'B2C',
    fullForm: 'Business to Consumer',
    meaning: 'Business selling directly to individual consumers.',
    whereSeen: 'Sales, marketing',
    bridge: 'Typically consumer-oriented buying journey.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'B2C businesses like retail, telecom and consumer apps deal with large volumes of individual customers, shorter decision cycles, and marketing that leans heavily on brand and emotional appeal rather than formal proposals. Teams working in B2C tend to obsess over conversion funnels and customer experience at scale, since a single individual customer rarely warrants dedicated account management the way a B2B client might.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It\'s closer to engaging directly with the general public rather than another organised body — think of community outreach or public-facing engagement where you\'re building broad awareness and trust with many individuals rather than negotiating formally with one counterpart organisation.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Remember this is B2C — the customer isn\'t going through a procurement process, they\'re deciding on impulse, so the messaging needs to be simple and immediate."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might over-formalise a B2C customer interaction — treating it like a structured negotiation with multiple approval stages — when the actual buying decision is fast, emotional and individual, requiring a very different communication style.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'D2C',
    fullForm: 'Direct to Consumer',
    meaning: 'Brand/manufacturer selling directly to consumers, often through digital channels.',
    whereSeen: 'Retail, FMCG, e-commerce',
    bridge: 'Reduces reliance on some intermediaries.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'D2C brands, common now in Indian FMCG and fashion, sell directly to consumers through their own website or app instead of going through traditional retail or distributor networks, which gives them more control over pricing, data and brand experience but also means they own the entire supply chain and customer service burden themselves. It\'s a frequent topic in retail strategy discussions because it changes the margin structure and the skills a company needs in-house.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This is somewhat like an organisation running its own direct supply and support line to the end user instead of routing everything through intermediate depots or distribution echelons — more control and speed, but also full responsibility for every link in that chain. Cutting out the middle layer means the originating unit now owns problems it used to be able to hand off.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Going D2C means we control the customer relationship end to end, but it also means we now own the delivery and returns headaches the distributor used to handle."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might assume D2C is simply "selling online," missing that its real significance is bypassing traditional distributor and retail intermediaries entirely, which shifts logistics, customer service and marketing responsibilities fully onto the brand.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'CTR',
    fullForm: 'Click-Through Rate',
    meaning: 'Percentage of users who click an advertisement, link or message after seeing it.',
    whereSeen: 'Digital marketing',
    bridge: 'A digital engagement metric.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'Click-through rate is one of the first numbers a digital marketing team looks at when judging whether an ad or email is actually catching attention, well before anyone worries about whether the click converts into a sale. A low CTR usually triggers changes to the creative or headline rather than the underlying offer, since it\'s treated as a signal about the ad\'s immediate appeal, not its ultimate effectiveness.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It\'s like measuring how many people who see a notice or briefing actually stop and engage with it further, as opposed to how many simply walk past — an early attention-capture metric rather than a measure of the eventual outcome. Getting attention and getting results are two separate questions.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"CTR on that banner is decent, but almost nobody who clicks is actually converting — the headline is doing its job, the landing page isn\'t."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might treat a strong CTR as proof the campaign is succeeding, not realising it only measures initial attention and says nothing about whether those clicks eventually turn into actual sales.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'CPC',
    fullForm: 'Cost Per Click',
    meaning: 'Amount paid or attributed for each advertising click.',
    whereSeen: 'Digital marketing',
    bridge: 'Used in performance advertising.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'Cost per click is the basic unit marketing teams use to judge how expensive it is to buy attention on a platform, and it\'s compared across channels and campaigns to decide where the advertising budget should be reallocated. Digital marketers watch CPC trends closely because rising CPC in a category — everyone bidding for the same keywords or audience — can quietly erode campaign profitability even while volume stays flat.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Think of it as the resource cost of securing each individual point of engagement or contact in a contested space — the more contested the space, the higher that cost climbs, and planners have to weigh whether continued engagement there is still worth it.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"CPC on that keyword has gone up sharply this month — we\'re bidding against more competitors for the same audience now."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might treat a low CPC as automatically good, without checking whether those cheap clicks are actually converting — cheap but irrelevant traffic can be worse value than more expensive but well-targeted traffic.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'CPM',
    fullForm: 'Cost Per Mille',
    meaning: 'Advertising cost per thousand impressions.',
    whereSeen: 'Digital marketing',
    bridge: 'A media pricing metric.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'CPM is used mainly for brand-awareness campaigns, where the goal is simply to get seen by as many people as possible rather than drive immediate clicks or sales, so media planners compare CPM across TV, digital display and outdoor to decide the most cost-effective way to buy visibility. It\'s a fundamentally different way of evaluating spend from CPC or CAC, and mixing them up in a budget conversation causes confusion.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It resembles the cost of ensuring a message or signal reaches a broad audience — a general information campaign or public messaging effort — where the objective is reach and exposure rather than any single individual response.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"For this brand campaign we\'re optimising for CPM, not clicks — the goal right now is just getting the name in front of as many eyeballs as possible."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might question why a campaign is being judged on CPM instead of conversions, not realising that awareness campaigns are deliberately evaluated on reach and cost-per-impression rather than immediate response.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'ROAS',
    fullForm: 'Return on Ad Spend',
    meaning: 'Revenue attributed to advertising relative to advertising spend.',
    whereSeen: 'Digital marketing',
    bridge: 'A campaign-level commercial efficiency metric.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'ROAS is the number performance marketing teams live and die by, because it directly ties advertising spend to revenue generated and gives leadership a clean way to decide whether to scale a campaign up or pull the plug. Campaigns with strong ROAS get more budget almost automatically in the next planning cycle, while weak-ROAS campaigns get cut regardless of how much effort went into producing the creative.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It\'s comparable to judging whether the resources committed to a particular operation actually produced results proportionate to that investment — a direct return-on-effort assessment used to decide whether to reinforce or scale back that specific line of effort next cycle.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our team\'s ROAS on that campaign came out at roughly three times spend, so leadership is comfortable putting more budget behind it next month."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might evaluate a marketing campaign on effort or creativity alone, not realising the decisive number leadership actually cares about is the direct revenue return relative to what was spent.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'SEO',
    fullForm: 'Search Engine Optimisation',
    meaning: 'Improving online content so it is more discoverable in search engines.',
    whereSeen: 'Digital marketing',
    bridge: 'Organic search visibility rather than paid advertising.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'SEO work is a slow, ongoing discipline — optimising website content, structure and technical factors so a company shows up higher in organic (unpaid) search results — and marketing teams treat it as a long-term investment quite separate from paid advertising campaigns. It\'s often undervalued by non-marketing colleagues because results take months to show, unlike a paid campaign that can be switched on and produce traffic immediately.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It resembles building sustained reputation and positioning over time rather than a quick, paid show of force — the equivalent of investing in long-term credibility and visibility so that others naturally come to you, rather than constantly having to actively project presence.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"SEO won\'t move the needle this month, but if we stop investing in it, we\'ll be paying for every single visitor through ads a year from now."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer accustomed to fast-turnaround operations might get impatient with SEO\'s slow timeline and push to abandon it in favour of paid ads, not appreciating that it\'s a long-horizon investment that compounds over time.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'SEM',
    fullForm: 'Search Engine Marketing',
    meaning: 'Search-engine marketing, often including paid search advertising; usage can vary.',
    whereSeen: 'Digital marketing',
    bridge: 'Confirm the organisation’s definition.',
    category: 'Sales & Marketing',
    indianContext: 'SEM often gets used loosely in Indian offices to mean paid search advertising specifically, even though strictly it can also include organic efforts — so a smart first move when the term comes up is simply to ask what the specific team or company means by it in that context. Marketing budgets are frequently split between SEM (paid) and SEO (organic) as two distinct line items, which is where the practical distinction matters most.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Because the exact scope shifts by organisation, it\'s a bit like a term that gets used slightly differently in different units — the wise move is always to clarify locally accepted usage rather than assume it means the same thing everywhere.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"When you say SEM budget, do you mean just the paid search spend, or are you including our organic search work too?"',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might assume SEM has one fixed, universal definition and use it confidently in a cross-team conversation, only to discover that the marketing team and the finance team in the same company are using it to mean slightly different things.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'SCM',
    fullForm: 'Supply Chain Management',
    meaning: 'Planning and management of sourcing, production, inventory, logistics and related flows.',
    whereSeen: 'Operations, manufacturing, logistics',
    bridge: 'A natural area of transfer for officers with logistics experience.',
    category: 'Operations, Supply Chain & Procurement',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'Supply Chain Management covers everything from raw material sourcing through production, warehousing and last-mile delivery, and in Indian manufacturing and logistics firms it\'s often the function where officers with a services logistics background land most naturally, given the overlap in planning, movement and inventory discipline. SCM leaders are judged on cost efficiency, on-time delivery and resilience to disruption all at once, which requires balancing trade-offs constantly.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'This maps very closely to overall logistics and sustainment planning — coordinating sourcing, movement, storage and distribution of materiel to keep operations running smoothly, with the same core tension between cost efficiency and readiness/resilience that any logistics planner recognises immediately.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our SCM team is under pressure to cut inventory costs, but every cut makes us more vulnerable if a supplier disruption hits — it\'s the classic trade-off."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer strong in tactical logistics execution might underestimate how much of corporate SCM is about long-term network design, vendor negotiation and cost optimisation across an entire commercial ecosystem, not just moving goods reliably from point A to point B.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'SKU',
    fullForm: 'Stock Keeping Unit',
    meaning: 'A unique identifier for a specific product/variant kept in inventory.',
    whereSeen: 'Retail, warehousing, FMCG',
    bridge: 'Think item-level inventory identity.',
    category: 'Operations, Supply Chain & Procurement',
    crossCategories: ['Retail, FMCG, E-commerce & Consumer'],
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'Every distinct product variant — different size, colour, flavour or pack — gets its own SKU code so retailers, warehouses and FMCG companies can track inventory, sales and replenishment at the most granular level. Conversations about "rationalising the SKU count" are common in retail and FMCG strategy meetings, since too many SKUs can bloat inventory costs and complicate operations even if each one sells reasonably well.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It\'s comparable to a unique stores or equipment code assigned to each distinct item type held in inventory, allowing precise tracking of exactly what is in stock, where, and in what quantity, down to the specific variant rather than just the general category.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We\'re carrying too many SKUs in that category — half of them barely sell and they\'re just tying up warehouse space."',
    trapForOfficers: 'An officer might think of inventory only at the category level ("we have enough soap"), not realising corporate inventory management tracks and makes decisions at the individual SKU level, where two variants of the same product can have very different demand patterns.',
