    whereSeen: 'Project plans, reviews',
    bridge: 'Used to track progress toward completion.',
    category: 'Project & Program Management',
    priorityTier: 1,
    indianContext: 'Project plans and steering committee reviews use milestones as checkpoints to show sponsors that a project is on track, often tied to phase payments or leadership visibility moments. Hitting or missing a milestone is usually the first thing a status report highlights.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It is comparable to phase lines or checkpoints within an operation, used to confirm the operation is proceeding to plan before the next phase begins.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We hit the milestone a week early, which should reassure the steering committee at Friday\'s review."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers might treat a milestone as if it were the final deliverable itself, when it is actually an intermediate checkpoint along the way, which can lead to premature reporting of "completion" to stakeholders.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Dependency',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'A task, decision, resource or external event that another activity relies upon.',
    whereSeen: 'Project reviews',
    bridge: 'Critical in cross-functional environments.',
    category: 'Project & Program Management',
    indianContext: 'Project reviews routinely flag dependencies across teams, vendors or other workstreams that could block progress if not resolved in time, and tracking them is a core part of a project manager\'s job. A missed dependency is one of the most common reasons a schedule slips.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It is comparable to relying on another unit\'s action or resource — engineering clearance, fire support, or a logistics resupply — before your own task can proceed as planned.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We can\'t start testing until the vendor dependency on the API integration is resolved."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may focus on visible, tangible dependencies (equipment, terrain) out of habit, while underestimating softer organisational dependencies like waiting on another department\'s sign-off, which are just as capable of stalling a corporate project.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Critical Path',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Sequence of activities that determines the minimum project duration under the plan.',
    whereSeen: 'Project management',
    bridge: 'Delay on critical-path activities can delay the overall project.',
    category: 'Project & Program Management',
    indianContext: 'Project managers use critical path analysis to identify exactly which sequence of tasks controls the overall finish date, so they can prioritise resources and attention there rather than spreading effort evenly across every task. It is a standard input into resourcing decisions during a project review.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It is comparable to the main axis of advance whose pace sets the tempo for the whole operation — delays there delay everything, while supporting tasks running in parallel usually have some slack.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Put the extra resources on the critical path tasks, the others have float and can wait a few days."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers might assume every urgent-looking task is on the critical path, when many tasks actually have slack and do not affect the overall end date, leading to effort misallocated toward non-critical work.',
