    whereSeen: 'Agile/Scrum',
    bridge: 'Typically measured in weeks, not months.',
    category: 'Project & Program Management',
    indianContext: 'Teams commit to a fixed few-week work cycle, review what got done at the end, and then plan the next one, treating the timebox itself as a firm discipline rather than something to extend informally. Sprint boards and burndown charts are a routine part of daily team life.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'It is comparable to a short, time-boxed phase of a longer operation with a clear objective for that phase, reviewed before the team moves on to planning the next phase.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"That item didn\'t get finished this sprint, let\'s carry it into the next one rather than extend this one."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers might treat "sprint" as informal jargon for working hard or rushing, when it actually denotes a fixed, calendar-boxed period with committed scope that the team is not expected to informally extend.',
