  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'HR',
    fullForm: 'Human Resources',
    meaning: 'Function responsible for people-related policies, talent, organisation and employee processes.',
    whereSeen: 'All companies',
    bridge: 'Corporate people-management function.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'In Indian companies HR is the day-to-day point of contact for onboarding, leave, payroll queries, grievance handling and policy interpretation, and its credibility often rests on how fairly and promptly it resolves individual cases. Officers transitioning will find that unlike a single adjutant or personnel section, HR in a large corporate is split into specialist sub-teams (recruitment, C&B, HRBP, L&D) that an employee has to figure out how to navigate individually. Line managers and employees alike route almost every people-related question through HR, so being on good terms with the local HR contact matters for getting things resolved quickly.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'HR is broadly analogous to the personnel/adjutant function that handles postings, records, welfare and administration for a unit, except in a corporation it also owns hiring, compensation design and policy-making rather than just executing centrally set rules. Where military personnel management runs on fairly standardised, service-wide regulations, corporate HR often has more local discretion and varies significantly company to company.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Raise a ticket with HR for the address change on your PF account — they usually turn it around in a couple of days."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers sometimes expect HR to have the same command-backed authority an adjutant\'s office has to simply direct outcomes, and get frustrated when HR instead facilitates, negotiates and follows a slower, more consultative process before decisions are finalized.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'HRBP',
    fullForm: 'Human Resources Business Partner',
    meaning: 'HR professional working closely with business leaders to align people practices with business needs.',
    whereSeen: 'Large corporates',
    bridge: 'A business-facing HR role.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'HRBPs are typically assigned to a specific business unit or function and attend leadership reviews, workforce planning discussions and performance calibration meetings as the "HR voice" embedded with that business, rather than sitting in a central HR office. Managers rely on their HRBP to translate business problems (attrition spikes, skill gaps, restructuring needs) into people interventions, and to flag people risks before they become business problems.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'An HRBP is somewhat like a personnel staff officer permanently attached to a formation who understands that formation\'s specific manning, morale and readiness issues in depth, rather than a generalist at a central records office who only sees the paperwork. The value lies in proximity and context, not just process knowledge.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Loop in the HRBP before you finalize the restructuring — she\'ll know which roles are sensitive from an attrition standpoint."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers new to the corporate world may treat the HRBP as a mere administrator to route forms through, missing that a good HRBP is expected to push back on a manager\'s plan and should be consulted early as a strategic partner, not informed after the decision is made.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'HRMS',
    fullForm: 'Human Resources Management System',
    meaning: 'Software supporting HR records and processes.',
    whereSeen: 'HR, employees',
    bridge: 'Employee information/process platform.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'Employees and managers use the HRMS for everything from applying leave and viewing payslips to updating personal details and initiating approvals, and its accuracy underpins payroll, compliance reporting and workforce analytics across the company. HR teams treat HRMS data as close to a single source of truth, so discrepancies (wrong reporting manager, missing joining date) can cascade into real payroll or access problems.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'HRMS functions much like a digitised version of a unit\'s personnel records and part-II orders system, holding the authoritative record of who is posted where, since when, and under what terms, except employees themselves can log in and directly update or request changes rather than routing everything through a records clerk.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Can you update your bank details in the HRMS yourself? Payroll pulls directly from whatever is showing there."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may assume someone else "owns" and maintains their record the way a unit orderly room does, and delay updating their own HRMS profile — not realizing that in most corporates the employee is personally responsible for keeping their own data current.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'HCM',
    fullForm: 'Human Capital Management',
    meaning: 'Broad approach/system covering workforce planning, talent, performance and HR processes.',
    whereSeen: 'HR, enterprise software',
    bridge: 'Broader than basic employee administration.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'HCM is the umbrella term leadership and HR technology vendors use when discussing workforce strategy holistically — covering hiring, performance, learning, succession and analytics as one connected system rather than isolated processes, and it shows up in vendor pitches, HR transformation projects and CHRO-level conversations. A company "investing in HCM" usually means integrating these previously separate HR functions onto one platform and one strategic narrative.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'HCM is comparable to treating personnel not just as records to administer but as a strategic resource to be planned, developed and deployed deliberately — similar in spirit to how manpower planning, training pipelines and career progression are meant to work together for long-term force readiness rather than each being managed in isolation.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our new HCM suite links performance ratings directly to the succession planning module, so calibration season should be less manual this year."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may mistake HCM for just another acronym for the HR software system, missing that it signals a philosophy — treating people development as a strategic, integrated capability — which matters when they\'re expected to speak to workforce strategy in a leadership conversation, not just system usage.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'ATS',
    fullForm: 'Applicant Tracking System',
    meaning: 'Software used to manage recruitment applications and hiring workflows.',
    whereSeen: 'Recruitment',
    bridge: 'The system behind many corporate hiring processes.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'Recruiters and hiring managers use the ATS to post openings, screen resumes (often with keyword filters before a human ever sees the profile), schedule interviews and track a candidate\'s status through each round, which is why career advice in India repeatedly stresses tailoring a resume\'s keywords to survive ATS screening. For officers applying to corporate roles, understanding that their resume may first be parsed by software rather than read by a person changes how they should write it.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'An ATS is somewhat like a centralized recruitment and selection board process compressed into software — screening, shortlisting and tracking candidates through stages — except the initial filtering is done by algorithm matching keywords rather than a panel reviewing a full service record and interview performance.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Your resume didn\'t even show up in the ATS shortlist — try reworking it with the exact skill terms from the job description."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers often write resumes in narrative, achievement-citation style drawn from military reports, not realizing an ATS scans for specific keywords and structured formatting, so a genuinely strong profile can get filtered out before a recruiter ever reads it.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'L&D',
    fullForm: 'Learning & Development',
    meaning: 'Activities supporting employee learning, capability and professional development.',
    whereSeen: 'HR, talent',
    bridge: 'Corporate equivalent of structured professional development, with business context.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'L&D teams design and run training calendars, e-learning platforms, leadership development programs and certification sponsorships, and employees typically have to actively request or enroll in these rather than have training assigned top-down as a matter of course. Managers are often expected to sponsor a direct report\'s L&D nomination, and completion of certain L&D milestones can factor into promotion readiness.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'L&D corresponds to the training and career-course ecosystem that develops officers through their service, but where military training courses are largely centrally mandated at fixed career points, corporate L&D is usually opt-in and employee-driven, requiring the individual to identify their own gaps and pursue development proactively.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Put in for the L&D budget before the quarter closes — you can use it for that data analytics certification you mentioned."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may wait to be nominated for training the way they would for a career course, when in most corporates they are expected to identify their own skill gaps and actively request L&D support rather than have it assigned to them.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'C&B',
    fullForm: 'Compensation & Benefits',
    meaning: 'Design and administration of pay, incentives and employee benefits.',
    whereSeen: 'HR, compensation',
    bridge: 'Often a specialist HR function.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'C&B specialists design salary structures, bonus and incentive plans, and benefits packages, and benchmark them against the market to keep the company competitive for talent — this team is who HR business partners consult on anything involving pay bands, increments or offer negotiations. Employees rarely interact with C&B directly but its decisions shape every CTC breakup and appraisal-linked payout they receive.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'C&B is broadly parallel to the function that designs pay scales, allowances and grade structures across ranks, except in a corporation these are periodically redesigned based on competitive market data rather than following a largely fixed, centrally legislated pay commission structure.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"C&B is reviewing the incentive structure for the sales team this quarter — the current payout formula isn\'t driving the right behaviour."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers coming from a fixed, rank-linked pay structure may assume their compensation package is largely non-negotiable, not realizing C&B-designed packages often have room for negotiation on components like joining bonus, ESOPs or variable pay.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'CTC',
    fullForm: 'Cost to Company',
    meaning: 'Annualised employer cost associated with an employee’s compensation package in India; exact components vary.',
    whereSeen: 'Indian job offers, HR',
    bridge: 'Do not equate CTC automatically with take-home salary.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'CTC is the headline number Indian employers quote in offer letters and appraisal discussions, but it typically bundles fixed pay, variable pay, employer PF contribution, insurance premiums and other non-cash benefits, meaning the actual monthly take-home is meaningfully lower than CTC divided by twelve. Candidates negotiating offers, and employees comparing raises, need to break down the CTC components rather than compare headline figures alone.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'CTC is a bit like comparing total emoluments including allowances, rations and accommodation value to a simple pay figure — the headline number in both cases includes items that are not cash directly in hand, so a like-for-like comparison requires looking past the top-line figure.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"The offer says 18 lakh CTC, but once you strip out the ESOP value and employer PF, the fixed take-home is closer to 12."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers moving from a system with relatively transparent, fully-disbursed pay may treat a quoted CTC figure as equivalent to actual annual take-home cash, and be caught off guard when the real in-hand salary is considerably lower once statutory deductions and non-cash components are excluded.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'ESOP',
    fullForm: 'Employee Stock Option Plan',
    meaning: 'Plan giving eligible employees options or rights linked to company shares, subject to plan terms.',
    whereSeen: 'Start-ups, listed/unlisted companies',
    bridge: 'An ownership-linked incentive, not ordinary salary.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'Start-ups and some listed companies offer ESOPs as part of the compensation package, particularly to attract talent when cash salaries can\'t match larger firms, and employees need to understand vesting schedules, strike price and liquidity terms before treating the stated ESOP value as real money in hand. HR and finance teams field frequent questions about when ESOPs actually convert to tradeable value, since this varies enormously by company stage and exit events.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'An ESOP is conceptually like being offered a stake in the long-term success of the mission rather than just a fixed reward for the current posting — its value depends entirely on the organisation succeeding and reaching a point (an IPO or acquisition) where that stake can be realized, so it carries real uncertainty unlike guaranteed pay.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"The offer includes 500 ESOPs vesting over four years — worth checking the strike price before you value that against a competing cash-heavy offer."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers unfamiliar with equity compensation may count the notional ESOP value at face value as guaranteed wealth, without realizing options are illiquid, subject to vesting cliffs, and could end up worth far less — or nothing — depending on company performance.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Attrition',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Employees leaving an organisation over a period, usually expressed as a rate.',
    whereSeen: 'HR dashboards, leadership reviews',
    bridge: 'Important workforce-health metric.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'Attrition rate is one of the first numbers HR presents in leadership reviews, since high attrition signals problems with pay competitiveness, manager quality, culture or workload, and drives urgent conversations about retention interventions for critical roles. Managers are often held accountable for their team\'s attrition, and repeated departures in a short window can trigger a formal review of that manager\'s people practices.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Attrition is comparable to tracking outflow through premature retirement requests, resignations or transfers-out from a unit — a rising rate is read as a signal of morale, leadership or working-conditions issues that need addressing before it affects operational readiness, much as high corporate attrition threatens delivery capability.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Attrition in the engineering team crossed our internal comfort threshold this quarter — we need to understand if it\'s compensation-related or a manager issue."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may assume attrition is purely a talent-management statistic to report upward, without realizing that as a people manager they are often personally accountable for their team\'s attrition number and expected to proactively diagnose and address its causes.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Retention',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Ability to keep employees or customers over time; in HR, usually employee retention.',
    whereSeen: 'HR, talent',
    bridge: 'The opposite side of the attrition/turnover question.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'Retention initiatives — retention bonuses, career conversations, internal mobility offers — are usually targeted specifically at high performers or critical-skill employees identified as flight risks, rather than applied uniformly across the workforce, and HRBPs typically maintain a watch list of such employees for proactive engagement. Line managers are expected to have regular career conversations as a retention tool, not just wait for a resignation letter to act.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Retention parallels efforts to retain trained, experienced personnel rather than losing them to premature exit — recognising that replacing an experienced hand costs far more in ramp-up time and lost institutional knowledge than the cost of proactively addressing what might make them leave.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We should have a retention conversation with her before the appraisal cycle — she\'s been getting external offers and we can\'t afford to lose that expertise."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may assume retention is something only HR manages centrally, when in practice a direct manager\'s ongoing engagement — recognition, growth conversations, workload fairness — is usually the frontline retention lever, and waiting for HR to intervene is often too late.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'EVP',
    fullForm: 'Employee Value Proposition',
    meaning: 'The overall value and experience an organisation offers employees in exchange for their contribution.',
    whereSeen: 'HR, employer branding',
    bridge: 'Why someone would join, stay and perform.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    priorityTier: 2,
    indianContext: 'EVP is the framing companies use in campus placements, employer branding campaigns and job postings to articulate why a candidate should choose them over competitors — covering pay, culture, growth opportunities, flexibility and purpose — and marketing/HR teams jointly craft and test this messaging. Internally, EVP also shapes what leadership tries to protect or improve when attrition rises, since a weakening EVP compared to competitors is often diagnosed as a root cause.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'EVP is somewhat like the combination of reasons — pride of service, camaraderie, training opportunities, post-retirement prospects — that make a career in uniform attractive and worth the sacrifices involved, except a corporation must actively articulate and market this proposition to compete for talent in an open labour market rather than relying on a largely captive recruitment pipeline.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"Our EVP messaging leans heavily on flexibility and learning opportunities — we should test whether that\'s actually what candidates in this segment care about."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers might dismiss EVP as marketing fluff, not recognising that articulating a compelling reason for people to join and stay is a genuine strategic exercise that leadership takes seriously when competing for scarce talent.',
