  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'PIP',
    fullForm: 'Performance Improvement Plan',
    meaning: 'Formal plan defining performance gaps, expectations, support and review period.',
    whereSeen: 'Performance management',
    bridge: 'A serious formal performance process; context matters.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'Being placed on a PIP is a formal, documented process with defined milestones and review dates, and in Indian corporates it often (though not always) signals that the company is building a paper trail either to support improvement or, if targets aren\'t met, to justify separation — so employees receiving one should treat it seriously rather than as routine coaching. Managers are trained to write clear, specific, measurable expectations in a PIP since vague plans are hard to defend if the outcome is later contested.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'A PIP is broadly like being placed under a formal review or a documented period of closer supervision after a below-par performance report, with specific milestones the individual must meet — the key parallel is that it\'s a formal, recorded process rather than an informal warning, and how the individual responds is closely tracked.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"HR wants the PIP document to include specific, measurable targets for each of the next three months, not just general feedback."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may read a PIP purely as a constructive coaching tool the way an informal counselling session might be, without recognising it is often a formally documented precursor to termination if targets aren\'t met, and needs to be treated with corresponding seriousness and possibly external advice.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Succession Planning',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Identifying and developing potential successors for important roles.',
    whereSeen: 'Leadership, HR',
    bridge: 'Similar to deliberate preparation for future command/leadership vacancies.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'Leadership and HR run succession planning as a periodic, often confidential exercise to identify internal candidates ready — or being groomed — for key leadership roles, so the organisation isn\'t caught exposed if a critical leader leaves suddenly. This shows up in talent review meetings where names are discussed against readiness timelines, and being identified as a "successor" is itself viewed as a career signal, sometimes communicated to the employee and sometimes not.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Succession planning is directly analogous to identifying and developing officers earmarked for future command or key staff appointments well ahead of the vacancy, ensuring there\'s a ready bench rather than a scramble when a senior position suddenly opens up.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"We flagged three names as ready-now successors for the regional head role in this year\'s talent review."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may assume succession planning conversations are always transparent and formally communicated the way postings and promotions boards are, when in many corporates these discussions happen confidentially at leadership level and an employee may never be told they are or aren\'t on the list.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Span of Control',
    fullForm: '—',
    meaning: 'Number of direct reports managed by a leader.',
    whereSeen: 'Organisation design, HR',
    bridge: 'Affects managerial bandwidth and structure.',
    category: 'HR & People',
    indianContext: 'Span of control comes up explicitly in organisation design discussions — when restructuring a team, HR and leadership debate whether a manager should have three direct reports or twelve, since too narrow a span wastes management overhead and too wide a span erodes the quality of coaching and oversight a manager can provide. It\'s a concrete design lever companies use when flattening or adding layers to the hierarchy.',
    militaryEquivalent: 'Span of control mirrors the same principle behind how many sub-units or personnel a commander can effectively direct and supervise directly — beyond a certain number, command effectiveness degrades, which is why intermediate layers of leadership exist in both settings.',
    dialogueSnippet: '"If we flatten this layer, the span of control for each team lead jumps to fifteen — that\'s probably too wide for the coaching we want them doing."',
    trapForOfficers: 'Officers may assume a wide span of control is simply a sign of efficiency or seniority, without appreciating that in a corporate context an unreasonably wide span is often flagged as a structural risk that undermines actual people management quality.',
  ),
  CorporateLanguageTerm(
    term: 'Competency',
